              "type": "text",
              "text": "A scientist shows a photomicrograph image of a 10 cm long cell.",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "break"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "He says the cell is magnified by a scale of ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "1:100\\,000",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " through his microscope.",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "break"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Explain what is wrong with the scientist’s ratio.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The ratio is reversed. Magnification should be $100\\,000:1$, since the image is larger than the actual cell.",
      "worked": "Scales are written in image : original order. A scale of $1:100\\,000$ means the image is smaller than the original, so it represents a reduction. For magnification by $100\\,000$, the ratio should be $100\\,000:1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
